## not-a-struct
[not-a-struct] `Nat` is not a struct, so it has no fields to read
primary: this field access requires a struct
help: change this value to a struct, or change/remove the field access

## type-mismatch
[type-mismatch] a natural number and a struct cannot be the same type
primary: Let’s call this shared value `Value` — the annotation fixes the type here
related: this expression supplies its type here; this is another requirement on `Value`
help: change the first use so it agrees with the other one
help: or change the other use so it agrees with the first one

## recursive-type
[recursive-type] this value would have to contain or accept itself
primary: Let’s call this function input `Input` — this call requires the input to accept itself
related: this input is required to be the value itself; this is another requirement on `Input`
help: change the call so a value is not passed to itself
help: or change the called value so it accepts a different input
note: 2 intermediate uses are omitted from this short explanation

## missing-field
[missing-field] no field `y` on `{ x: Nat }`
primary: this use conflicts over field `y`
help: allow field `y`, or change the use that requires it

## extra-field-struct
[extra-field] field `y` is required by one use but excluded by another
primary: Let’s call this function input `Input` — this use limits the type so field `y` is unavailable
related: this use requires field `y`; this is another requirement on `Input`
help: allow field `y` in the limiting use
help: or change the use that requires field `y`
note: 1 intermediate use is omitted from this short explanation

## extra-field-sum
[extra-field] case `#None` is required by one use but excluded by another
primary: Let’s call this function input `Input` — this use limits the type so case `#None` is unavailable
related: this use requires case `#None`; this is another requirement on `Input`
help: allow case `#None` in the limiting use
help: or change the use that requires case `#None`
note: 1 intermediate use is omitted from this short explanation

## rigid-broken
[rigid-broken] this is `{ value: Nat }`, but `'a` stands for whatever type the caller picks
primary: this use narrows a choice that belongs to the caller
related: declared here
help: change the body so it works for every choice allowed by the annotation

## rigid-field-struct
[rigid-field] this reads field `x`, but `'a` stands for whatever other struct fields the caller chooses, so `x` cannot be assumed
primary: this assumes one of the struct fields chosen by the caller
related: the caller's choice of struct fields starts here
help: change the body so it does not assume which struct fields the caller chooses

## rigid-field-sum
[rigid-field] this matches case `#B`, but `'r` stands for whatever other cases the caller chooses, so `#B` cannot be assumed
primary: this assumes one of the cases chosen by the caller
related: the caller's choice of cases starts here
help: change the body so it does not assume which cases the caller chooses

## rigid-field-effect
[rigid-field] this requires effect `!Log`, but `'effects` stands for whatever other effects the caller chooses, so `!Log` cannot be assumed
primary: this assumes one of the effects chosen by the caller
related: the caller's choice of effects starts here
help: change the body so it does not assume which effects the caller chooses

## rigid-escapes
[rigid-escapes] `'a` stands for whatever the caller picks, so it can't be part of a type outside the annotation that declared it
primary: this type carries a caller choice outside its annotation
help: keep the value inside the annotation that introduces this choice

## repeated-field-struct
[repeated-field] field `x` cannot be both named and included by `..`
primary: this `..` remainder already follows named field `x`
related: this use introduces field `x`
help: remove field `x` from the named side
help: or keep it out of the `..` remainder
note: 3 intermediate uses are omitted from this short explanation

## repeated-field-sum
[repeated-field] case `#A` cannot be both named and included by `..`
primary: this `..` remainder already follows named case `#A`
related: this use introduces case `#A`
help: remove case `#A` from the named side
help: or keep it out of the `..` remainder
note: 3 intermediate uses are omitted from this short explanation

## repeated-field-effect
[repeated-field] `..` covers only the effects a type does not already name, and here it would have to cover `!Log`
primary: effect `!Log` is already named outside `..`
help: remove the repeated effect, or remove it from the remainder

## presence-required
[presence-required] this value needs `x != y` among its fields, and it does not have that
primary: this value does not meet the required combination
help: change the value or loosen the required combination

## presence-impossible
[presence-impossible] nothing can satisfy `'x != 'y`: what this definition does with the type has already ruled it out
primary: the annotation and definition rule out every combination
help: change the annotation or change how the definition uses the value

## clause-impossible
[clause-impossible] nothing can satisfy `'x and not 'x`: this clause rules out every value at once
primary: this clause rules out every combination
help: remove one of the conflicting requirements in this clause

## annotation-allows-more
[annotation-allows-more] the annotation allows `'x or 'y`, but the definition requires `'x != 'y`
primary: this annotation promises more combinations than the body accepts
help: strengthen the annotation or loosen the body

## unhandled-effect
[unhandled-effect] nothing can handle `!Log` here: a definition's value is computed outside every handler
primary: effect `!Log` has no enclosing handler
help: handle this effect, or perform it inside a function

## effect-not-allowed
[effect-not-allowed] this function performs `!Log`, which its type does not allow
primary: effect `!Log` is not listed by this function
help: add the effect to the function type, or handle it here

## callback-effects-not-covered
[callback-effects-not-covered] this callback may perform effects the containing extern call does not allow
primary: this callback may perform effects the extern call does not allow
help: allow those effects on the extern call, or handle them inside the callback

## polymorphic-extern-boundary
[polymorphic-extern-boundary] host code needs one fixed kind of value at this extern position
primary: host code needs one fixed kind of value at this position
help: use a fixed type here, or keep the generic value behind a Ruddy wrapper

## repeated-calls
[type-mismatch] a natural number and a boolean cannot be the same type
primary: Let’s call this function input `Input` — this argument supplies a type to the call
related: this call fixes what type the argument must have; this is another requirement on `Input`
related: this argument supplies a type to the call; this is another requirement on `Input`
help: change the first use so it agrees with the other one
help: or change the other use so it agrees with the first one
note: 2 intermediate uses are omitted from this short explanation

## branches
[type-mismatch] a natural number and a struct cannot be the same type
primary: Let’s call this branch result `Result` — this branch contributes its type to the match result
related: this branch contributes its type to the match result; this is another requirement on `Result`
help: change the first use so it agrees with the other one
help: or change the other use so it agrees with the first one

## non-function
[type-mismatch] a natural number cannot be called as a function
primary: this expression is called as a function
help: change the first use so it agrees with the other one
help: or change the other use so it agrees with the first one

